    "available_at": "2026-11-16T23:10:07.452345+00:00",
    "body": "( Stock image by Kaliel. )\nPosted By: Mining.com Staff November 16, 2026\nTwo of China’s leading rare earth producers more than doubled their profits in the first nine months of 2026 as tighter supply controls supported higher prices.\nThe country’s biggest light rare earth producer, China Northern Rare Earth (SSE: 600111), reported net profit attributable to shareholders of 3.65 billion yuan (US$547.2 million), up 136.6% from a year earlier, according to its third-quarter results.\nChina Rare Earth Resources and Technology (SZSE: 000831), the main listed arm of China Rare Earth Group, posted net profit attributable to shareholders of 408.1 million yuan (US$61.2 million) for the same period, up 112.4%.\n“Supply-side reforms have helped support prices by improving industry discipline,” Citi analyst Anna Wang told Reuters .\nGovernment efforts\nThe earnings gains show how Beijing’s efforts to curb oversupply and consolidate production are benefiting domestic producers. Export restrictions have also become a bargaining tool in trade talks with the United States.\nPrices for neodymium-praseodymium (NdPr) oxide, used in permanent magnets, have risen 62% since China introduced rare earth export controls in April 2025, to 719,000 yuan (US$107,800) per tonne, according to Shanghai Metals Market data cited by Reuters .\nChina allocated its first batch of 2024 mining and smelting-separation quotas to China Northern Rare Earth and China Rare Earth Group, the parent of the Shenzhen-listed company.\nRules introduced in July 2025 explicitly brought the smelting and separation of imported rare earth ore under production quotas. They also require producers to report monthly and annual quota use and enter product-flow information in a tracking system each month.\nBoth listed producers are on track to exceed analysts’ full-year profit estimates, Reuters reported Nov. 16.\nChina’s long game\nChina has long used production quotas and industry consolidation to manage rare earth supply.\nRare earths are used in permanent magnets and defence equipment. The sector’s price swings have squeezed producers’ profits.\nChina’s export controls cover selected medium and heavy rare earths and magnets containing them. NdPr oxide is outside the April 2025 restrictions.\nThe United States already has commercial rare earth production. MP Materials (NYSE: MP) produced 1,018 tonnes of NdPr oxide in the third quarter, up 41% from a year earlier, and said saleable magnet deliveries to General Motors would begin in the fourth quarter.\n(With files from Reuters) Print\nTAG: China rare earth elements rare earths\nCOMMODITY: Rare Earths\nREGION: United States\nRepublish this article",
